From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jens Willmer <info@jwillmer.de>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 12:05:10 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 76/86] Clones never lose unsaved text to a mirror

Three ways a mirrored edit could overwrite work without warning:

- Two tabs of one file with different unsaved text (drafts of a restored
  session) were made equal, keeping one draft. They are now left apart,
  each with its own text, and no longer mirror each other until one is
  closed; a warning names the file.
- Reload (from the changed-on-disk bar) and Revert all changes in one clone
  pushed the file's text over the other's edits as a mirrored change. They
  now reload every clone of the file, as one action.
- A mirrored edit changed text hidden in a fold of the other tab. It now
  unfolds what it touches first, as an edit typed in that tab does.

The mirror tests set up the syntax system themselves, so they also pass
when run alone.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

